{
  "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
  "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Microsoft Corporation",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The limited monopoly granted by U.S. patent laws protects against the making, using or selling a patented invention within the United States. Section 271(f) also makes it an infringement to make components of a patented invention within the United States and then simply ship them abroad for reassembly."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice SCALIA: Mr. Olson, before you get into the merits I have a question, a preliminary question. I understand from AT&T's brief that there has been a stipulation entered into between the parties after the judgment below which preserved Microsoft's right to appeal and prescribed different dollar amounts that Microsoft must pay AT&T depending on the outcome of the appeal. Does that raise any, any muteness problem? Can you sort of wager on the outcome of an appeal that way?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: What, what is your view of what the component is in this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. JoSEFFER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. JOSEFFER: The component is the, is the actual machine readable copy of software that is inserted in --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. JoSEFFER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. JOSEFFER: The United States view -- I'm not sure exactly how much we disagree on this -- but the United States view is that, for example, a blank disk is not a component of this invention because you don't need a blank disk to practice this invention."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I take it that we are operating under the assumption that software is patentable? We have never held that in this Court, have we?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Can I ask you, the point I don't understand when you say the -- the component is the disk that contains material, I understand that because here is a separate piece; it's a disk. You plug it in; it has the information on it. But then you say, if you put it on the hard drive it becomes the component."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: That, that code is not patentable, you've said."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: The physical object does not have to originate in the United States. Can they not transmit the commands to a physical object in Germany and have that be the substitute for the golden disk?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: But is there any precedent for that sort of thing?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, is it an answer to Justice Breyer, or maybe it isn't, that we have no conceptual problem saying there would be liability if this happened within the territorial limits of the United States?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Waxman (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It -- it makes liable as an infringer somebody who supplies -- who is in the United States who supplies from the United States a component with the intent, with the express intent that that component be combined in a way to create a device that would practice a U.S. patent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Waxman (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "c58ebfde40f8738c91b181ec051c000295c7e16843233b1b62db797c490409af",
      "evidence_id": "ev-c58ebfde40f8-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One, this is something that is totally modular. It is developed, bought and sold entirely independent of any of the hardware to which it is, with which it is combined, and between which it moves continuously as it operates. Number two, it can be removed or updated entirely independently of the other components. And it is dynamic, unlike designs, unlike molds, unlike instructions about how to make something, all of which are exhausted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2006-05-1056",
      "confidence": 0.0,
